Go guests are built with the Go release pinned in ../sources/go/default.nix
and the patch series beside it. default.nix and go.sh build that tree with
Go's own make.bash, bootstrapped by nixpkgs' Go, into a Go installation.

Two settings select what the series adds to Go's wasip1 port. The wasix
build tag is the WASIX ABI: sockets, name resolution and an epoll poller.
GOWASM=threads adds WASIX threads: a runtime on OS threads through
thread_spawn and futexes, in a module with shared memory and atomic
instructions. The toolchain's environment sets both.

A program built with both is a wasip1 module that listens on the port it
asks for, dials out where the runtime's policy allows, resolves names, and
runs its goroutines on as many threads as GOMAXPROCS says, by default the
count the runtime reports. Its threads block in WASIX futexes and epoll
when idle, as a freeze needs them to.

A Go service's package passes `toolchain = sc.goToolchain` to
sc.mkGuestBuild and declares two sources: its upstream tree, and the
modules that tree's go.mod names, vendored by sc.fetchGoModules. Its
build.sh copies the modules to vendor/ inside the tree and runs the go
command through sc_go, which keeps the build cache inside the build
directory. The toolchain's environment sets GOOS, GOARCH, GOWASM, the wasix
tag and vendor mode, and turns fetching off, so an untouched tree builds
with

    sc_go -C "$tree" build -o "$SC_BUILD_DIR/server.wasm" ./cmd/server

Nix caches the built toolchain; each service build compiles the standard
library for wasip1 into its own cache.

`./x go` links the installation at work/build/go-toolchain, for building a
tree by hand. Its servicecache.env holds the environment a service build
sets; a tree that fetches its modules needs only the target and the two
settings:

    export PATH="$PWD/work/build/go-toolchain/bin:$PATH"
    export GOOS=wasip1 GOARCH=wasm GOWASM=threads
    go build -tags wasix -o server.wasm ./cmd/server
    toolchain/run-wasix.sh server.wasm

`./x go --test` runs Go's own tests of the packages the series changes,
through test.sh: the wasip1 packages as guests of the Wasmer CLI built from
source, and the compiler, assembler and linker packages natively. The
source guests are built from leaves Go's test files out, so the tests run
with the same toolchain built from the release with its tests. test-skips
lists the tests left out, each with its reason.

The series is developed on the wasix branch of a Go checkout, work/go by
default, and written back with export-patches.sh.
